import Mathlib
import CryptoLean.OpenProblems.OneWay

/-!
# Knot theory open problems (ProofAtlas geometry & topology list)

None of the problems below is solved here. This file builds a small combinatorial knot layer
on **braid words** and states, as unproved `def … : Prop`s, the problems whose objects are
definable from it, plus the slice–ribbon conjecture in a smooth Euclidean model (`S³ ⊂ ℝ⁴`);
the others are recorded in the table at the end of this comment.

## The combinatorial model

* A *braid word* on `n` strands is a list of nonzero integers `a` with `|a| < n`; the letter
  `i > 0` is the Artin generator `σ_i` (strands `i` and `i+1` cross, a positive crossing when
  all strands are oriented in the same direction) and `-i` is `σ_i⁻¹`.
* The *closure* of a braid word is a closed braid diagram; it is a knot (one component) iff the
  strand permutation is transitive (`IsKnotBraid`).
* `MarkovEquiv` is the equivalence relation generated by the moves of the braid-group
  presentation (free cancellation, far commutation, the braid relation, all in context),
  cyclic rotation (which together with free cancellation generates conjugation) and Markov
  stabilisation `(n, w) ~ (n+1, w σ_n^{±1})`.

**Known theorems that are used for the modelling but are not formalized here.**
Alexander's theorem (every tame oriented link in `S³` is the closure of a braid) and Markov's
theorem (two braids have ambient-isotopic oriented closures iff they are related by braid-group
equality, conjugation and stabilisation) identify `MarkovEquiv` classes of braid words with
oriented tame link types in `S³`; the unknot is the class of the empty word on one strand. So
"`K` is the unknot" is modelled faithfully (for tame = smooth knots) by
`MarkovEquiv (n, w) (1, [])`. For singular knots we use the singular-braid analogue of
Alexander's theorem (Birman 1993, *New points of view in knot theory*, Bull. AMS 28).

* `kauffmanBracket n w` is Kauffman's state sum of the closed braid diagram, normalised by
  `⟨O⟩ = 1`, as an element of Mathlib's `LaurentPolynomial ℤ` in the variable `A`, using
  `⟨σ_i⟩ = A·⟨id⟩ + A⁻¹·⟨e_i⟩`, `⟨σ_i⁻¹⟩ = A⁻¹·⟨id⟩ + A·⟨e_i⟩`, and `δ = -A² - A⁻²` per extra loop.
* `jonesA n w = (-A³)^{-writhe} ⟨w⟩` is the Jones polynomial in the variable `A`; the Jones
  polynomial in `t` is obtained by the substitution `A = t^{-1/4}` (Kauffman 1987), which is
  injective, so `V_K = 1 ↔ jonesA n w = 1`.

## Open problems stated as `Prop`s

| rank | problem | declaration |
|------|---------|-------------|
| 242 | Vassiliev separation by abelian-valued finite-type invariants | `VassilievSeparation` |
| 289 | Slice–ribbon conjecture | `SliceRibbonConjecture` |
| 290 | Jones unknot conjecture | `JonesUnknotConjecture` |
| 294 | Polynomial-time unknot recognition | `PolyTimeUnknotRecognition` |

## Open problems not expressible faithfully with Mathlib today

| rank | problem | missing Mathlib objects |
|------|---------|-------------------------|
| 114 | Volume conjecture for knots | colored Jones polynomials `J_{K,N}` (Jones–Wenzl projectors / `U_q(sl₂)` representations), hyperbolic structures on knot complements and their hyperbolic volume |
| 160 | L-space conjecture | closed 3-manifolds / rational homology spheres as topological objects with `π₁`, Heegaard Floer homology (L-spaces), coorientable taut foliations; (left-orderable groups alone are definable) |
| 346 | Chen–Yang volume conjecture for Turaev–Viro invariants | Turaev–Viro state-sum invariants (triangulations of 3-manifolds with quantum `6j`-symbols), hyperbolic 3-manifolds (closed, cusped, totally geodesic boundary) and their volume |
| 438 | Meridional rank conjecture | (the link group itself *is* definable: Mathlib's `FundamentalGroup` of the complement of the image of an embedding into `Metric.sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) 1`) — missing are the **meridians** of a link component (the boundary-of-a-normal-disk loop, which needs tubular neighbourhoods of submanifolds, absent from Mathlib), hence the meridional generating number `μ(L)`; **generic (Morse) height functions** restricted to a smooth link; and the **bridge number** `b(L)` as a minimum over ambient-isotopic smooth embeddings (no ambient isotopy of submanifolds in Mathlib) |

## Known results proved here (finite checks)

* the empty one-strand braid and the stabilised unknot `σ₁` on two strands have bracket and
  Jones polynomial `1`, and `(2, σ₁)` is Markov-equivalent to the unknot;
* the right-handed trefoil `σ₁³` has Kauffman bracket `-A⁵ - A⁻³ + A⁻⁷` and Jones polynomial
  `A⁻⁴ + A⁻¹² - A⁻¹⁶` (that is, `V = t + t³ - t⁴`), in particular `≠ 1`;
* the figure-eight knot `σ₁σ₂⁻¹σ₁σ₂⁻¹` has Jones polynomial `A⁸ - A⁴ + 1 - A⁻⁴ + A⁻⁸`
  (`V = t⁻² - t⁻¹ + 1 - t + t²`), in particular `≠ 1`;
* consequently the Jones unknot conjecture *as stated* (an `↔`) implies that the trefoil and
  the figure-eight braids are not Markov-equivalent to the unknot (an implication only; the
  invariance of the Jones polynomial under Markov moves is a known theorem not formalized here);
* constant functions are Vassiliev invariants of order `0` (`const_hasOrderAtMost_zero`).
-/

namespace CryptoLean.OpenProblems.GeometryTopology.Knots

open LaurentPolynomial
open scoped ContDiff

/-! ## Braid words, closures, Markov equivalence -/

/-- A braid word on `n` strands: nonzero letters `a` with `|a| < n` (`a = ±i` is `σ_i^{±1}`). -/
def ValidWord (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : Prop := ∀ a ∈ w, a ≠ 0 ∧ a.natAbs < n

instance (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : Decidable (ValidWord n w) := by
  unfold ValidWord; infer_instance

/-- Where the strand at position `j` goes after the letter `a` (a transposition of the
positions `|a|-1` and `|a|`, counted from `0`). -/
def letterMap (a : ℤ) (j : ℕ) : ℕ :=
  if j + 1 = a.natAbs then a.natAbs else if j = a.natAbs then a.natAbs - 1 else j

/-- The strand permutation of a braid word, as a map on positions. -/
def strandMap (w : List ℤ) (j : ℕ) : ℕ := w.foldl (fun p a => letterMap a p) j

/-- The closure of the braid word `w` on `n ≥ 1` strands is a knot (has one component): the
strand permutation is transitive on `{0, …, n-1}`. (An orbit of a permutation of an `n`-set is
traversed in fewer than `n` steps, so bounding `k < n` loses nothing.) -/
def IsKnotBraid (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : Prop :=
  1 ≤ n ∧ ValidWord n w ∧ ∀ i < n, ∀ j < n, ∃ k < n, (strandMap w)^[k] i = j

instance (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : Decidable (IsKnotBraid n w) := by
  unfold IsKnotBraid; infer_instance

/-- One elementary move between braid words (with strand numbers). -/
inductive MarkovStep : ℕ × List ℤ → ℕ × List ℤ → Prop
  /-- insert a cancelling pair `σ σ⁻¹` -/
  | free (n : ℕ) (u v : List ℤ) (a : ℤ) :
      ValidWord n (u ++ v) → a ≠ 0 → a.natAbs < n →
      MarkovStep (n, u ++ v) (n, u ++ a :: -a :: v)
  /-- far commutation `σ_i^{±} σ_j^{±} = σ_j^{±} σ_i^{±}` for `|i - j| ≥ 2` -/
  | far (n : ℕ) (u v : List ℤ) (a b : ℤ) :
      ValidWord n (u ++ a :: b :: v) → a.natAbs + 2 ≤ b.natAbs ∨ b.natAbs + 2 ≤ a.natAbs →
      MarkovStep (n, u ++ a :: b :: v) (n, u ++ b :: a :: v)
  /-- the braid relation `σ_i σ_{i+1} σ_i = σ_{i+1} σ_i σ_{i+1}` -/
  | braid (n : ℕ) (u v : List ℤ) (i : ℕ) :
      ValidWord n (u ++ v) → 1 ≤ i → i + 1 < n →
      MarkovStep (n, u ++ (i : ℤ) :: ((i + 1 : ℕ) : ℤ) :: (i : ℤ) :: v)
        (n, u ++ ((i + 1 : ℕ) : ℤ) :: (i : ℤ) :: ((i + 1 : ℕ) : ℤ) :: v)
  /-- cyclic rotation `a u ~ u a` (conjugation by `a`) -/
  | rotate (n : ℕ) (a : ℤ) (u : List ℤ) :
      ValidWord n (a :: u) → MarkovStep (n, a :: u) (n, u ++ [a])
  /-- Markov stabilisation `(n, w) ~ (n+1, w σ_n^{±1})` -/
  | stab (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) (ε : ℤ) :
      1 ≤ n → ValidWord n w → (ε = 1 ∨ ε = -1) →
      MarkovStep (n, w) (n + 1, w ++ [ε * n])

/-- Markov equivalence of braid words: by Alexander's and Markov's theorems (not formalized),
the closures are ambient-isotopic oriented links in `S³`. -/
def MarkovEquiv : ℕ × List ℤ → ℕ × List ℤ → Prop := Relation.EqvGen MarkovStep

/-! ## The Kauffman bracket and the Jones polynomial of a closed braid -/

/-- All smoothing states of `m` crossings (`true` = keep the strands vertical, the `id`
smoothing; `false` = the cup–cap smoothing `e_i`). -/
def allStates : ℕ → List (List Bool)
  | 0 => [[]]
  | m + 1 => (allStates m).flatMap fun s => [true :: s, false :: s]

/-- `A`-exponent of a state: `σ_i` contributes `+1` for `id`, `-1` for `e_i`;
`σ_i⁻¹` the opposite. -/
def stateExp : List ℤ → List Bool → ℤ
  | a :: w, b :: s => (if (0 < a) = b then 1 else -1) + stateExp w s
  | _, _ => 0

/-- Union of the classes of `x` and `y` in a labelling. -/
def unite (lab : List ℕ) (x y : ℕ) : List ℕ :=
  let lx := lab.getD x 0
  let ly := lab.getD y 0
  lab.map fun z => if z = ly then lx else z

/-- The edges of the smoothed closed braid diagram. Vertex `k * n + j` is position `j` just
above crossing `k` (level `k`, indices mod `m`); crossing `k` with letter `a` joins level `k`
to level `k+1`. -/
def smoothingEdges (n m : ℕ) : ℕ → List ℤ → List Bool → List (ℕ × ℕ)
  | k, a :: w, b :: s =>
      let i := a.natAbs - 1
      let top := fun j => k * n + j
      let bot := fun j => ((k + 1) % m) * n + j
      let others := ((List.range n).filter fun j => j ≠ i ∧ j ≠ i + 1).map fun j => (top j, bot j)
      let here := if b then [(top i, bot i), (top (i + 1), bot (i + 1))]
        else [(top i, top (i + 1)), (bot i, bot (i + 1))]
      others ++ here ++ smoothingEdges n m (k + 1) w s
  | _, _, _ => []

/-- Number of loops of the state `s` of the closure of the braid word `w` on `n` strands. -/
def loops (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) (s : List Bool) : ℕ :=
  if w.length = 0 then n else
    (((smoothingEdges n w.length 0 w s).foldl (fun lab e => unite lab e.1 e.2)
      (List.range (w.length * n))).dedup).length

/-- The data `(A-exponent, number of loops)` of every state. -/
def stateData (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : List (ℤ × ℕ) :=
  (allStates w.length).map fun s => (stateExp w s, loops n w s)

/-- The loop value `δ = -A² - A⁻²`. -/
noncomputable def δ : LaurentPolynomial ℤ := -T 2 - T (-2)

/-- The Kauffman bracket of the closed braid diagram, normalised by `⟨O⟩ = 1`:
`⟨D⟩ = Σ_states A^{a(s) - b(s)} δ^{loops(s) - 1}`. -/
noncomputable def kauffmanBracket (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : LaurentPolynomial ℤ :=
  ((stateData n w).map fun p => T p.1 * δ ^ (p.2 - 1)).sum

/-- Writhe of a closed braid: the sum of the crossing signs. -/
def writhe (w : List ℤ) : ℤ := (w.map Int.sign).sum

/-- The Jones polynomial in the variable `A`: `f(D) = (-A³)^{-w(D)} ⟨D⟩`. The Jones polynomial
`V(t)` is `f` with `A = t^{-1/4}`. -/
noncomputable def jonesA (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : LaurentPolynomial ℤ :=
  (-1) ^ (writhe w).natAbs * T (-3 * writhe w) * kauffmanBracket n w

/-! ## Rank 290: the Jones unknot conjecture -/

/-- **Jones unknot conjecture** (OPEN; ProofAtlas #290, "Jones Unknot Conjecture").
ProofAtlas exactTarget: *"Prove or disprove: a knot K in S^3 has Jones polynomial V_K = 1 if
and only if K is the unknot (equivalently, every nontrivial knot has nontrivial Jones
polynomial)."*

Modelling simplifications: knots are tame and given as closures of braid words (Alexander's
theorem); "`K` is the unknot" is `MarkovEquiv (n, w) (1, [])` (Markov's theorem); the Jones
polynomial is `jonesA`, i.e. `V_K` in the variable `A = t^{-1/4}` via Kauffman's state sum, so
`V_K = 1 ↔ jonesA n w = 1`. The `←` direction is the (known, not formalized here) invariance
of the Jones polynomial; the open content is `→`. Stated only; NOT proved. -/
def JonesUnknotConjecture : Prop :=
  ∀ n w, IsKnotBraid n w → (jonesA n w = 1 ↔ MarkovEquiv (n, w) (1, []))

/-! ## Rank 294: polynomial-time unknot recognition -/

/-- Binary encoding of a braid word: `n` in unary, then each letter as a sign bit followed by
its absolute value in unary. Its length is polynomial in the number of strands and crossings
of the closed braid diagram. -/
def encodeBraid (n : ℕ) (w : List ℤ) : List Bool :=
  List.replicate n true ++ [false] ++
    w.flatMap fun a => decide (0 < a) :: (List.replicate a.natAbs true ++ [false])

/-- **Polynomial-time unknot recognition** (OPEN; ProofAtlas #294, "Polynomial-time unknot
recognition"). ProofAtlas exactTarget: *"Determine whether there is a polynomial-time
algorithm that, given a knot diagram or an equivalent finite encoding of a knot in
three-space, decides whether the knot is the unknot."*

This `Prop` is the positive answer: a deterministic polynomial-time Turing machine (Mathlib
`TM2`, via `CryptoLean.OpenProblems.OneWay.PolyTimeDecider`) decides, on the encoding of any
braid word whose closure is a knot, whether that knot is the unknot. Modelling
simplifications: input knots are encoded as braid words (`encodeBraid`) rather than planar
diagrams; this is polynomially equivalent (a closed braid is a diagram, and Vogel's algorithm
turns a diagram with `c` crossings into a braid word of size polynomial in `c` in polynomial
time — a known theorem not formalized here); the unknot is identified via Alexander's and
Markov's theorems as above; inputs that are not knot braids are a promise-excluded case, which is harmless because
`IsKnotBraid` (transitivity of the strand permutation of `w`) is itself decidable in time
polynomial in `|encodeBraid n w|`, so any decider can first reject non-knot braids.
The problem asks to *determine* whether this `Prop` holds; it is stated only, NOT decided. -/
def PolyTimeUnknotRecognition : Prop :=
  ∃ M : List Bool → Bool, CryptoLean.OpenProblems.OneWay.PolyTimeDecider M ∧
    ∀ n w, IsKnotBraid n w → (M (encodeBraid n w) = true ↔ MarkovEquiv (n, w) (1, []))

/-! ## Rank 242: Vassiliev separation -/

/-- Letters of singular braid words: an ordinary letter `σ_i^{±1}` or a double point `τ_i`
between strands `i` and `i+1`. -/
inductive SLetter
  | gen (a : ℤ)
  | dbl (i : ℕ)

/-- Validity of a singular letter on `n` strands. -/
def SLetter.Valid (n : ℕ) : SLetter → Prop
  | .gen a => a ≠ 0 ∧ a.natAbs < n
  | .dbl i => 1 ≤ i ∧ i < n

/-- The strand transposition letter underlying a singular letter. -/
def SLetter.toLetter : SLetter → ℤ
  | .gen a => a
  | .dbl i => i

/-- Whether a singular letter is a double point. -/
def SLetter.isDbl : SLetter → Bool
  | .gen _ => false
  | .dbl _ => true

/-- Number of double points of a singular braid word. -/
def numDbl (s : List SLetter) : ℕ := s.countP SLetter.isDbl

/-- The closure of the singular braid word `s` on `n` strands is a singular knot. -/
def IsSingularKnotBraid (n : ℕ) (s : List SLetter) : Prop :=
  (∀ x ∈ s, x.Valid n) ∧ IsKnotBraid n (s.map SLetter.toLetter)

/-- All resolutions of the double points, with signs: `τ_i ↦ σ_i` (sign `+`) and
`τ_i ↦ σ_i⁻¹` (sign `-`), the Vassiliev skein relation `v(K_×) = v(K_+) - v(K_-)`. -/
def resolutions : List SLetter → List (ℤ × List ℤ)
  | [] => [(1, [])]
  | .gen a :: s => (resolutions s).map fun p => (p.1, a :: p.2)
  | .dbl i :: s => (resolutions s).flatMap fun p => [(p.1, (i : ℤ) :: p.2), (-p.1, -(i : ℤ) :: p.2)]

/-- `v` is an invariant of (oriented) knot types: constant on Markov classes of knot braids. -/
def IsKnotInvariant {A : Type*} (v : ℕ × List ℤ → A) : Prop :=
  ∀ x y : ℕ × List ℤ, IsKnotBraid x.1 x.2 → MarkovEquiv x y → v x = v y

/-- The extension of `v` to singular braid words by the alternating resolution relation. -/
def extendSingular {A : Type*} [AddCommGroup A] (v : ℕ × List ℤ → A) (n : ℕ)
    (s : List SLetter) : A :=
  ((resolutions s).map fun p => p.1 • v (n, p.2)).sum

/-- `v` is a Vassiliev invariant of order at most `d`: its extension vanishes on every singular
knot (closure of a singular braid word) with `d + 1` double points. -/
def HasOrderAtMost {A : Type*} [AddCommGroup A] (d : ℕ) (v : ℕ × List ℤ → A) : Prop :=
  ∀ n s, IsSingularKnotBraid n s → numDbl s = d + 1 → extendSingular v n s = 0

/-- **Vassiliev separation by abelian-valued finite-type invariants** (OPEN; ProofAtlas #242).
ProofAtlas exactTarget: *"For every pair of non-isotopic smooth closed oriented unframed knots
K_1,K_2 in S^3, assert that there exist an abelian group A, a nonnegative integer d and an
A-valued Vassiliev invariant v of order at most d with v(K_1) != v(K_2). Knots are taken up to
ambient isotopy respecting their orientation, so a knot and its reverse are included when they
are distinct oriented knot types. Extend v linearly from the free abelian group on oriented
knot types to singular knots by the usual alternating positive/negative crossing-resolution
relation; order at most d means vanishing on every singular knot with d+1 transverse double
points. The coefficient group and finite order may depend on the pair. Rational-valued,
integer-valued and Kontsevich-injectivity variants are related but are not asserted
equivalent here."*

Modelling simplifications: oriented knots (and their types) are closures of braid words up to
`MarkovEquiv` (Alexander and Markov theorems; braid closures carry the braid orientation, so
a knot and its reverse are distinct when they are distinct oriented types); singular knots are
closures of singular braid words (Birman's singular Alexander theorem); `v` is a function on
braid words that is constant on Markov classes of knot braids; its extension resolves every
double point `τ_i` as `σ_i - σ_i⁻¹`. `A : Type` (universe 0) with an `AddCommGroup` structure.
Stated only; NOT proved. -/
def VassilievSeparation : Prop :=
  ∀ n₁ w₁ n₂ w₂, IsKnotBraid n₁ w₁ → IsKnotBraid n₂ w₂ → ¬ MarkovEquiv (n₁, w₁) (n₂, w₂) →
    ∃ (A : Type) (_ : AddCommGroup A) (d : ℕ) (v : ℕ × List ℤ → A),
      IsKnotInvariant v ∧ HasOrderAtMost d v ∧ v (n₁, w₁) ≠ v (n₂, w₂)

/-! ## Rank 289: the slice–ribbon conjecture -/

/-- A smooth knot in `S³ = Metric.sphere (0 : ℝ⁴) 1`, as a `1`-periodic `C^∞` immersion
`k : ℝ → ℝ⁴` with values in `S³` that is injective on one period (equivalently a smooth
embedding `S¹ → S³`). -/
def IsSmoothKnot (k : ℝ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) : Prop :=
  ContDiff ℝ ∞ k ∧ Function.Periodic k 1 ∧ Set.InjOn k (Set.Ico 0 1) ∧
    (∀ t, deriv k t ≠ 0) ∧ ∀ t, k t ∈ Metric.sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) 1

/-- `f` restricted to the closed unit disk `D² ⊂ ℝ²` is a smooth, properly embedded disk in the
4-ball `B⁴ = closedBall 0 1 ⊂ ℝ⁴` with boundary the knot `k`: `f` is `C^∞` on `D²` (one-sided
derivatives on `∂D²`), injective with injective derivative on `D²` (an injective immersion of a
compact space, hence an embedding), maps `∂D²` onto the image of `k` in `S³ = ∂B⁴` and the open
disk into the open ball (properness), and is transverse to `S³` along `∂D²` (the outward radial
derivative of `f` has nonzero radial component). -/
def IsSliceDisk (k : ℝ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2) →
    EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) : Prop :=
  ContDiffOn ℝ ∞ f (Metric.closedBall 0 1) ∧ Set.InjOn f (Metric.closedBall 0 1) ∧
    (∀ x ∈ Metric.closedBall (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) 1,
      Function.Injective (fderivWithin ℝ f (Metric.closedBall 0 1) x)) ∧
    (∀ x ∈ Metric.closedBall (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) 1,
      ‖f x‖ ≤ 1 ∧ (‖f x‖ = 1 ↔ ‖x‖ = 1)) ∧
    (∀ x ∈ Metric.sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) 1,
      inner ℝ (f x) (fderivWithin ℝ f (Metric.closedBall 0 1) x x) ≠ 0) ∧
    f '' Metric.sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) 1 = Set.range k

/-- A *ribbon disk* for `k`: a slice disk on which the squared radial function `‖f ·‖²` is Morse
in the interior (every interior critical point has a nondegenerate Hessian) and has no interior
local maximum. -/
def IsRibbonDisk (k : ℝ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2) →
    EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 4)) : Prop :=
  IsSliceDisk k f ∧ ∀ x ∈ Metric.ball (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) 1,
    fderiv ℝ (fun y => ‖f y‖ ^ 2) x = 0 →
      (∀ v, v ≠ 0 → ∃ w, fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ (fun y => ‖f y‖ ^ 2)) x v w ≠ 0) ∧
        ¬ IsLocalMax (fun y => ‖f y‖ ^ 2) x

/-- **Slice–ribbon conjecture** (OPEN; ProofAtlas #289, "Slice-Ribbon Conjecture").
ProofAtlas exactTarget: *"Is every smoothly slice knot in S^3 (one that bounds a smoothly
embedded disk in the 4-ball) a ribbon knot (one bounding an immersed disk in S^3 with only
ribbon singularities)?"*

This `Prop` is the positive answer: every smooth knot in `S³` that bounds a smooth properly
embedded disk in `B⁴` (`IsSliceDisk`) bounds a ribbon disk (`IsRibbonDisk`).
Modelling simplifications, all documented:
* `S³`, `B⁴`, `D²` are the unit sphere/balls in Euclidean space and smoothness on the closed
  disk is `ContDiffOn` on `closedBall 0 1` (Mathlib has no manifold-with-boundary instance on
  `D²` or `B⁴` and no notion of properly embedded submanifold; properness and transversality to
  `∂B⁴` are written out by hand);
* *ribbon* is modelled not by immersed disks in `S³` with ribbon singularities (not in Mathlib)
  but by the classical equivalent characterization: a knot is ribbon iff it bounds a smooth
  slice disk in `B⁴` on which the radial function is Morse with no local maxima (a known
  theorem, see e.g. Gompf–Stipsicz, *4-Manifolds and Kirby Calculus*, §6.2, and Kawauchi,
  *A Survey of Knot Theory*, §12; not formalized here).
The answer is not known; this is stated only, NOT proved. -/
def SliceRibbonConjecture : Prop :=
  ∀ k, IsSmoothKnot k → (∃ f, IsSliceDisk k f) → ∃ f, IsRibbonDisk k f

/-! ## Known results: finite computations -/

lemma T_mul_δ (a : ℤ) : T a * δ = -T (a + 2) - T (a + -2) := by
  simp only [δ, mul_sub, mul_neg, ← T_add]

lemma T_mul_δ_sq (a : ℤ) : T a * δ ^ 2 = T (a + 4) + 2 * T a + T (a + -4) := by
  have h1 : T a * δ ^ 2 = (T a * δ) * δ := by ring
  have h2 : (-T (a + 2) - T (a + -2)) * δ = -(T (a + 2) * δ) - T (a + -2) * δ := by ring
  rw [h1, T_mul_δ, h2, T_mul_δ, T_mul_δ]
  have e1 : a + 2 + 2 = a + 4 := by ring
  have e2 : a + 2 + -2 = a := by ring
  have e3 : a + -2 + 2 = a := by ring
  have e4 : a + -2 + -2 = a + -4 := by ring
  rw [e1, e2, e3, e4]; ring

lemma T_mul_T (a b : ℤ) : (T a : LaurentPolynomial ℤ) * T b = T (a + b) := (T_add a b).symm

/-- Finite check: state data of the trefoil braid `σ₁³` on two strands. -/
theorem stateData_trefoil :
    stateData 2 [1, 1, 1] =
      [(3, 2), (1, 1), (1, 1), (-1, 2), (1, 1), (-1, 2), (-1, 2), (-3, 3)] := by decide

/-- Finite check: state data of the figure-eight braid `σ₁σ₂⁻¹σ₁σ₂⁻¹` on three strands. -/
theorem stateData_figureEight :
    stateData 3 [1, -2, 1, -2] =
      [(0, 3), (-2, 2), (2, 2), (0, 1), (-2, 2), (-4, 3), (0, 1), (-2, 2), (2, 2), (0, 1),
        (4, 3), (2, 2), (0, 1), (-2, 2), (2, 2), (0, 1)] := by decide

/-- Known result (finite check): the trivial one-strand braid (the standard unknot diagram)
has Kauffman bracket `1` and Jones polynomial `1`. -/
theorem jonesA_unknot : kauffmanBracket 1 [] = 1 ∧ jonesA 1 [] = 1 := by
  have h : stateData 1 [] = [(0, 1)] := by decide
  have hk : kauffmanBracket 1 [] = 1 := by
    simp [kauffmanBracket, h, T_zero]
  refine ⟨hk, ?_⟩
  simp [jonesA, hk, writhe, T_zero]

/-- Known result (finite check): the stabilised unknot `σ₁` on two strands (a diagram of the
unknot with one kink) has Kauffman bracket `-A³` and Jones polynomial `1`. -/
theorem jonesA_kink : kauffmanBracket 2 [1] = -T 3 ∧ jonesA 2 [1] = 1 := by
  have h : stateData 2 [1] = [(1, 2), (-1, 1)] := by decide
  have hk : kauffmanBracket 2 [1] = -T 3 := by
    simp only [kauffmanBracket, h, List.map, List.sum_cons, List.sum_nil, Nat.reduceSub,
      pow_one, pow_zero, mul_one, T_mul_δ, Int.reduceAdd, Int.reduceNeg]
    ring
  refine ⟨hk, ?_⟩
  have hw : writhe [1] = 1 := by decide
  rw [jonesA, hk, hw]
  have e : ((1 : ℤ).natAbs) = 1 := rfl
  rw [e, pow_one, mul_neg, neg_mul, neg_mul, neg_neg, one_mul, T_mul_T]
  norm_num [T_zero]

/-- Known result: `(2, σ₁)` is Markov-equivalent to the trivial one-strand braid (one
stabilisation); so the stabilised braid closure is the unknot. -/
theorem markovEquiv_kink_unknot : MarkovEquiv (2, [1]) (1, []) := by
  have h := MarkovStep.stab 1 [] 1 le_rfl (by decide) (Or.inl rfl)
  norm_num at h
  exact Relation.EqvGen.symm _ _ (Relation.EqvGen.rel _ _ h)

/-- Known result (finite check): the Kauffman bracket of the right-handed trefoil `σ₁³` is
`-A⁵ - A⁻³ + A⁻⁷` (Kauffman 1987, *State models and the Jones polynomial*, Topology 26). -/
theorem kauffmanBracket_trefoil :
    kauffmanBracket 2 [1, 1, 1] = -T 5 - T (-3) + T (-7) := by
  simp only [kauffmanBracket, stateData_trefoil, List.map, List.sum_cons, List.sum_nil,
    Nat.reduceSub, pow_one, pow_zero, mul_one, T_mul_δ, T_mul_δ_sq, Int.reduceAdd, Int.reduceNeg]
  ring

/-- Known result (finite check): the Jones polynomial of the right-handed trefoil in the
variable `A` is `A⁻⁴ + A⁻¹² - A⁻¹⁶`, i.e. `V(t) = t + t³ - t⁴` (Jones 1985). -/
theorem jonesA_trefoil : jonesA 2 [1, 1, 1] = T (-4) + T (-12) - T (-16) := by
  have hw : writhe [1, 1, 1] = 3 := by decide
  rw [jonesA, kauffmanBracket_trefoil, hw]
  have e : ((3 : ℤ).natAbs) = 3 := rfl
  have e9 : (-3 * 3 : ℤ) = -9 := by norm_num
  rw [e, e9]
  have h : (-1 : LaurentPolynomial ℤ) ^ 3 * T (-9) * (-T 5 - T (-3) + T (-7)) =
      T (-9) * T 5 + T (-9) * T (-3) - T (-9) * T (-7) := by ring
  rw [h, T_mul_T, T_mul_T, T_mul_T]
  norm_num

/-- Known result (finite check): the figure-eight knot `σ₁σ₂⁻¹σ₁σ₂⁻¹` has Jones polynomial
`A⁸ - A⁴ + 1 - A⁻⁴ + A⁻⁸`, i.e. `V(t) = t⁻² - t⁻¹ + 1 - t + t²` (Jones 1985). -/
theorem jonesA_figureEight :
    jonesA 3 [1, -2, 1, -2] = T 8 - T 4 + 1 - T (-4) + T (-8) := by
  have hw : writhe [1, -2, 1, -2] = 0 := by decide
  have e : ((0 : ℤ).natAbs) = 0 := rfl
  rw [jonesA, hw, e, pow_zero, mul_zero, T_zero, one_mul, one_mul]
  simp only [kauffmanBracket, stateData_figureEight, List.map, List.sum_cons, List.sum_nil,
    Nat.reduceSub, pow_one, pow_zero, mul_one, T_mul_δ, T_mul_δ_sq, Int.reduceAdd, Int.reduceNeg]
  rw [T_zero]
  ring

/-- Finite check: the closures of `σ₁³` and `σ₁σ₂⁻¹σ₁σ₂⁻¹` are knots (one component). -/
theorem isKnotBraid_examples : IsKnotBraid 2 [1, 1, 1] ∧ IsKnotBraid 3 [1, -2, 1, -2] := by
  decide

/-- Finite check: the Jones polynomials of the trefoil and the figure-eight are not `1`. -/
theorem jonesA_trefoil_ne_one : jonesA 2 [1, 1, 1] ≠ 1 ∧ jonesA 3 [1, -2, 1, -2] ≠ 1 := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    rw [jonesA_trefoil, ← T_zero] at h
    have := congrArg (fun p : LaurentPolynomial ℤ => p.coeff (-4)) h
    simp only [AddMonoidAlgebra.coeff_add, AddMonoidAlgebra.coeff_sub, Finsupp.add_apply,
      Finsupp.sub_apply, T_apply] at this
    norm_num at this
  · intro h
    rw [jonesA_figureEight, ← T_zero] at h
    have := congrArg (fun p : LaurentPolynomial ℤ => p.coeff 8) h
    simp only [AddMonoidAlgebra.coeff_add, AddMonoidAlgebra.coeff_sub, Finsupp.add_apply,
      Finsupp.sub_apply, T_apply] at this
    norm_num at this

/-- Implication: the Jones unknot conjecture as stated (with its `↔`) implies that neither the
trefoil nor the figure-eight braid is Markov-equivalent to the unknot. Only the known direction
of the `↔` (Markov equivalence preserves the Jones polynomial) is used, and the conclusion is a
known theorem; this is a consistency check of the statement, not progress on the open
direction. -/
theorem not_unknot_of_jonesUnknotConjecture (h : JonesUnknotConjecture) :
    ¬ MarkovEquiv (2, [1, 1, 1]) (1, []) ∧ ¬ MarkovEquiv (3, [1, -2, 1, -2]) (1, []) :=
  ⟨fun hm => jonesA_trefoil_ne_one.1 ((h _ _ isKnotBraid_examples.1).2 hm),
   fun hm => jonesA_trefoil_ne_one.2 ((h _ _ isKnotBraid_examples.2).2 hm)⟩

/-- Sanity check that `JonesUnknotConjecture` is not vacuous on the unknot side: the kinked
unknot braid satisfies both sides of the `↔`. -/
theorem jonesUnknot_kink_instance :
    IsKnotBraid 2 [1] ∧ jonesA 2 [1] = 1 ∧ MarkovEquiv (2, [1]) (1, []) :=
  ⟨by decide, jonesA_kink.2, markovEquiv_kink_unknot⟩

lemma sum_flatMap_pm (l : List (ℤ × List ℤ)) :
    (l.flatMap fun p => [p.1, -p.1]).sum = 0 := by
  induction l with
  | nil => simp
  | cons x l ih => simp [List.flatMap_cons, ih]

lemma sum_signs_resolutions (s : List SLetter) (h : 1 ≤ numDbl s) :
    ((resolutions s).map Prod.fst).sum = 0 := by
  induction s with
  | nil => simp [numDbl] at h
  | cons x s ih =>
    cases x with
    | gen a =>
      have h' : 1 ≤ numDbl s := by simpa [numDbl, SLetter.isDbl] using h
      simpa [resolutions, List.map_map, Function.comp_def] using ih h'
    | dbl i =>
      simp only [resolutions, List.map_flatMap]
      simpa using sum_flatMap_pm (resolutions s)

lemma sum_map_fst_smul {A : Type*} [AddCommGroup A] (l : List (ℤ × List ℤ)) (c : A) :
    (l.map fun p => p.1 • c).sum = (l.map Prod.fst).sum • c := by
  induction l with
  | nil => simp
  | cons x l ih => simp [ih, add_smul]

/-- Known result (easy): constant functions are Vassiliev invariants of order `0`
(the alternating resolution sum of a constant vanishes once there is a double point). -/
theorem const_hasOrderAtMost_zero {A : Type*} [AddCommGroup A] (c : A) :
    HasOrderAtMost 0 (fun _ : ℕ × List ℤ => c) := by
  intro n s _ hd
  unfold extendSingular
  rw [sum_map_fst_smul, sum_signs_resolutions s (by omega), zero_smul]

